'''Whangārei''' (formerly '''Whangarei''') is a New Zealand parliamentary [[New Zealand electorates|electorate]] that was first created for the {{NZ election link|1972}}. The electorate is usually a reasonably safe [[New Zealand National Party|National]] seat, and was held for long periods by [[John Banks (New Zealand politician)|John Banks]] ({{NZ election link year|1981}}–1999) and [[Phil Heatley]] ({{NZ election link year|1999}}–2014), before being won in the {{NZ election link|2014}} by [[Shane Reti]]. In the {{NZ election link|2020}} Reti narrowly lost the seat to Labour's [[Emily Henderson (politician)|Emily Henderson]]. Reti would reclaim the seat at the {{NZ election link|2023}} with a huge majority.

==Population centres==
Since the {{NZ election link|1969}}, the number of electorates in the [[South Island]] was fixed at 25, with continued faster population growth in the [[North Island]] leading to an increase in the number of general electorates. There were 84 electorates for the 1969 election,{{sfn|McRobie|1989|p=111}} and the 1972 electoral redistribution saw three additional general seats created for the North Island, bringing the total number of electorates to 87.{{sfn|McRobie|1989|p=115}} Together with increased urbanisation in [[Christchurch]] and [[Nelson, New Zealand|Nelson]], the changes proved very disruptive to existing electorates.{{sfn|McRobie|1989|p=115}} In the South Island, three electorates were abolished, and three electorates were newly created.{{sfn|McRobie|1989|pp=112, 116}} In the North Island, five electorates were abolished, two electorates were recreated, and six electorates were newly created (including Whangārei).{{sfn|McRobie|1989|pp=111, 115}}

{{maplink|from=New Zealand Electorates/2020/General/Whangārei.map|frame=yes|text=Map of boundaries at the [[2020 New Zealand general election|2020]] and [[2023 New Zealand general election|2023]] general elections}}
The boundaries of the Whangārei electorate were adjusted for the {{NZ election link|1999}}; before then, the electorate bordered onto the [[Kaipara Harbour]]. Redistributions in 2002,<ref>{{cite book| title=Report of the Representation Commission 2002 |url= http://www.elections.org.nz/sites/default/files/2002%20Representation%20Commission%20Report.pdf |isbn=0-478-20169-9 |publisher=Representation Commission |access-date=4 October 2014 |page=8 |date=21 March 2002}}</ref> 2007,<ref>{{cite book| title=Report of the Representation Commission 2007 |url= http://www.elections.org.nz/sites/default/files/2007%20Representation%20Commission%20Report.pdf |isbn=978-0-477-10414-2 |publisher=Representation Commission |access-date=4 October 2014 |page=8 |date=14 September 2007}}</ref> and 2013/14<ref>{{cite book| title=Report of the Representation Commission 2014 |url= http://www.elections.org.nz/sites/default/files/bulk-upload/documents/report_of_the_representation_commission_2014.pdf |isbn=978-0-477-10414-2 |publisher=Representation Commission |access-date=4 October 2014 |page=8 |date=4 April 2014}}</ref> did not change the boundaries further. Changes ahead of the {{NZ election link|2020}} moved [[Poroti]] and [[Maungakaramea]] into the [[Northland (New Zealand electorate)|Northland]] electorate<ref>{{cite web|url=https://www.vote.nz/map/index.html?id=2&modified=2020110110227 |title=Map of electorates for the 2020 and 2023 elections |date=17 April 2020}}</ref> and also changed the electorate's name from Whangarei to Whangārei. Redistributions for the [[2026 general election]] saw the electorate extend northwards to the [[Whangaruru]] harbour.<ref>{{cite book |title=Report of the Representation Commission 2025 |url=https://elections.nz/assets/pagecomponent-file-files/REPORT-OF-THE-REPRESENTATION-COMMISSION-2025-WEB.pdf |isbn=978-0-473-75233-0 |publisher=Representation Commission |access-date=3 September 2025 |page=20 |date=8 August 2025 }}</ref>

The electorate includes the following population centres:
* [[Whangārei]]
* [[Hikurangi]]
* [[Ruakaka]]
* [[Whananaki]]
* [[Ōakura, Northland|Ōakura]]
* [[Whangaruru]]
It extends from [[Whangaruru]] in the north to Ruatangata and [[Maungatapere]] in the west and [[Waipu, New Zealand|Waipu]] and Langs Beach in the south.

==History==
The electorate was created for the {{NZ election link|1972}}. It was won by the Labour Party in that election, but was then held by the National Party until 2020. [[Phil Heatley]] held it from {{NZ election link year|1999}}<ref name="Heatley parliament bio">{{cite web |title=Hon Phil Heatley |url= http://www.parliament.nz/en-nz/mpp/mps/former/50PlibMpsFormerPhilHeatley1/heatley-hon-phil |publisher=[[New Zealand Parliament]] |access-date=21 October 2014 |date=22 September 2014}}</ref> until he retired in 2014.<ref name="MP Phil Heatley Will Retire from Politics">{{cite news |last=Vance |first=Andrea |url= http://www.stuff.co.nz/taranaki-daily-news/news/national/9351677/MP-Phil-Heatley-will-retire-from-politics |title=MP Phil Heatley to Retire |date=1 November 2013 |work=[[Stuff (website)|Stuff]] |access-date=1 November 2013}}</ref> [[Shane Reti]] stood for National in the {{NZ election link|2014}} and had a large margin over Labour's Kelly Ellis.<ref>{{cite news |last=Watkins |first=Tracy |title=Labour announces Chch Central candidate |url= http://www.stuff.co.nz/the-press/news/city-centre/9808282/Labour-announces-Chch-Central-candidate |access-date=10 March 2014 |work=[[The Press]] |date=10 March 2014 |page=A2}}</ref><ref name="2014 election">{{cite news |title=Election 2014: Shane Reti wins Whangarei |url= http://www.nzherald.co.nz/nz/news/article.cfm?c_id=1&objectid=11328607 |access-date=27 September 2014 |work=[[The New Zealand Herald]] |date=20 September 2014}}</ref>

